id: pm-ouyq3n
title: "The relationship lexicon cannot express recurrence, so a defect family fixed thirty times is thirty unrelated nodes to every graph algorithm"
description: "Verified live 2026-08-03 by asking the product to accept the relation and reading its refusal.\n\nThe accepted dependency-kind domain is fifteen spellings: blocks, parent, child, parent_child, child_of, related, related_to, discovered_from, blocked_by, incident_from, epic, implements, supersedes, task, verifies. None of them means \"this is the same defect again\". The closest candidates are all something else. supersedes means replacement, and the replaced thing is expected to stop existing; a recurrence does not supersede its predecessor, because the predecessor was correctly fixed. discovered_from means derivation of knowledge, not repetition of a failure. incident_from means an item was raised from an incident and carries three rows in the entire corpus. related is undirected and carries no semantics at all.\n\nThe measured consequence is severe. A defect family that has been diagnosed and fixed roughly thirty times over eight months exists in the graph as roughly thirty disconnected items. Graph traversal cannot reach the second occurrence from the first. Ancestors, descendants, impact, dominators, centrality and community detection all see thirty independent defects. Every analytic the project has built for understanding its own history is blind to the single most expensive pattern in that history.\n\nThere is a live conformance gap. A feature filed the same day specifies a recurrence analyzer whose acceptance criteria require it to fold \"typed recurrence or supersedes edges\" into versioned defect families. Typed recurrence edges cannot be written. The feature therefore cannot be satisfied without either extending the lexicon or silently reinterpreting supersedes, and reinterpreting supersedes would corrupt the fifty-seven existing rows that mean replacement.\n\nThe gap is a missing relation, not a missing canonicalization and not a missing promotion pass. The lexicon item owns collapsing fifteen spellings onto one canonical spelling per relation, and must land first so that a new relation is added to a governed vocabulary rather than a sixteenth ungoverned spelling. The lineage item owns promoting evolution statements that already exist in durable text onto the kinds that already exist. Neither creates a relation that does not exist, and neither can, because the promotion rule has no target kind to promote recurrence into.\n\nRecurrence also needs semantics the existing kinds do not carry: it is directional and time-ordered, it must survive the closure of both endpoints, it must be assertable between items with different root causes but the same observable failure, and it must be distinguishable from a duplicate. A duplicate says two records describe one event. A recurrence says two events share an identity."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: graph,ontology,recurrence
created_at: "2026-08-03T20:34:50.941Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-16T15:08:15.339Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-16T15:08:14.680Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-16T15:08:14.680Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 420
acceptance_criteria: "A recurrence relation exists in the declared dependency-kind domain with stated direction, time ordering, and terminal-endpoint validity, and is distinguishable from supersedes and from duplicate; The relation is added to the canonical vocabulary rather than as a sixteenth ungoverned spelling, so the lexicon consolidation lands first and the new kind is defined against the consolidated set; Graph traversal, impact, dominators and community detection reach every occurrence of a family from any other occurrence, proved on the historical platform-regression family; The existing supersedes rows keep replacement semantics unchanged and no row is retyped by the introduction of the new relation; The recurrence analyzer feature can satisfy its typed-recurrence acceptance criterion without reinterpreting any existing kind"
parent: pm-96h7
risk: high
confidence: 88
resolution: "Registered recurs_from as directed, persistent, many-to-many, non-self, source-after-target, and non-ordering; enriched an eight-generation live recurrence chain."
expected_result: The canonical relationship vocabulary can distinguish a later recurrence from its earlier defect and expose that temporal family to every graph consumer without treating it as execution precedence.
actual_result: "recurs_from is a first-class directed non-ordering relationship with source_after_target chronology enforced before persistence for create, update, and update-many, including dry-run and equal-timestamp refusal with structured endpoint evidence."
